  "id": "waveshare-p4-module-devkit",
  "name": "Waveshare ESP32-P4-Module-DEV-KIT",
  "short": "ESP32-P4-Module-DEV-KIT",
  "chip": "ESP32-P4",
  "notes": "Raspberry-Pi-sized board with an ESP32-P4-Module (ESP32-P4NRW32: 32 MB in-package PSRAM, 16 MB flash, plus an ESP32-C6 for Wi-Fi 6/BLE over ESP-Hosted SDIO) and 100M Ethernet (IP101, PoE module header). Flash and use the console through the Type-C port marked USB TO UART (CH343P bridge on UART0 GPIO37/38, auto-reset via DTR/RTS). The other Type-C port, marked USB, is the P4's USB 1.1 Serial/JTAG on GPIO24/25. The 40-pin header is one 2x20 Pi-style block on the board edge, not two side rows. Here left = outer row (board edge: 5V, 5V, GND, TXD...), right = inner row (3V3, SDA, SCL...), listed from the RJ45/USB-A end down to the button end. That is Waveshare's board photo orientation; the header pinout picture is drawn rotated 180 degrees. The Type-C ports are on the long side, near the bottom in this orientation. GPIO54 (header pin 32) is the C6's CHIP_PU/reset and GPIO6 (pin 16) is wired to C6 GPIO2 inside the module, so neither is free. GPIO0/1 carry a 32.768 kHz crystal inside the module. GPIO45 (pin 39) switches the microSD power (low = on), and GPIO53 (pin 34) enables the speaker amplifier. GPIO39-48 are powered by the P4's internal LDO VO4. GPIO32/33 have 2.2k pull-ups (the I3C SH1.0 connector). There is no user LED, only a power LED. The BOOT button is GPIO35, which is also Ethernet TXD1, so there is no page button.",
